 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to requiring children under the age of 8 to be restrained by a child restraint system in a motor vehicle",
    "basePrintNo": "A8612",
    "basePrintNoStr": "A8612-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1229-c, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A8612\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation\nto  requiring  children  under  the age of 8 to be restrained by a child\nrestraint system in a motor vehicle\n\nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill would enhance highway safety for children who  are  passengers\nin motor vehicles.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection  1  of  the  bill would amend Vehicle and Traffic Law (\"VTL\")\n1229-c(1)(b) and (c) to raise from seven years to eight years the  maxi-\nmum  age  at  which  children  over  the  age of four are required to be\nrestrained in booster seats while they are passengers in the  back  seat\nof an automobile. This section would also require that all other passen-\ngers  in  the  back  seat,  regardless of age, be restrained by a safety\nbelt.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend and  renumber  VTL  §  1229-c(3-a)  to\nclarify  that a passenger must wear both a lap belt, and a shoulder belt\nacross the torso, when sitting in a vehicle.\n\nSections 3 and 4 of the bill would make conforming amendments to VTL\n1229-c(6)  and  (12), respectively, concerning the increased maximum age\nof rear seat child passengers set forth in section 1 of the bill.\n\nSection 5 of the bill would provide that the bill would take  effect  90\ndays after it becomes law.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nCurrently,  the  VTL requires only children between the ages of four and\nseven to be restrained in child passenger restraint systems. There is no\nspecific requirement that a shoulder harness belt  be  worn  across  the\ntorso.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA.11083-A,  which  passed the Assembly in 2007, contained the provisions\nset forth in bill sections 1, 3, and 4 of this bill.\n\nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nPursuant to two recent enactments (Chapter 509 of the Laws of  2004  and\nChapter  18  of the Laws of 2005), children between the ages of four and\nseven are required to be restrained in special  booster  seats,  as  are\nthose under four who weigh more than forty pounds. However, according to\nthe Center for Disease Control growth charts, the average eight-year-old\nchild  weighs  about  68  pounds,  while 95% of children under age eight\n\nweigh less than 80 pounds. All current booster seats are  certified  for\nat  least  80  pounds  and many, for up to 100 pounds. The average eight\nyear old is about 50\" tall (4 feet 2 inches) and 96% of this  age  group\nare  under  54\"  (4  feet  6  inches).  Due to the difference in anatomy\nbetween adults and children, the recommended height to use an adult seat\nbelt is 4 feet 9 inches. Therefore,  increasing  the  age  for  required\nseating in an appropriate child restraint system (\"booster seat\") recog-\nnizes  the  reality  of  the  physical  size of this age group, and will\nenhance the safety of these children when  they  are  passengers  in  an\nautomobile.\n\nThe  bill's  provision  concerning  the proper placement of the shoulder\nharness safety belt is in response to  a  court  decision  dismissing  a\nticket  for  a violation of VTL § 1229-c where the defendant was wearing\nthe shoulder harness under her arm. The court held that this was  not  a\nviolation  of  the  law  because  VTL § 1229-c (3-a) does not explicitly\nrequire that the belt be worn over the shoulder, but only that the wear-\ner be \"restrained\". (People v. Locke, 1 Misc.  3d  482  (Watertown  City\nCourt  2003)).  Clearly,  the  intent of existing law is that a shoulder\nharness safety belt be worn across the torso, in order that the belt may\nbe effective. This bill would explicitly delineate that intention, so as\nto properly and effectively restrain and  protect  motor  vehicle  occu-\npants.\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis bill would take effect 90 days after becoming law.\n\n",
